            "creation_time": "2026-11-22T19:58:12Z",
            "count": 0,
            "text": "## Summary\n\n`tools::parse_Rd()` leaks the lexer's token buffer when an R error is raised while a token longer than 128 bytes is being read, 16 KB per call for a macro name over 10000 bytes. Fixes a reproducible leak, low impact: it only happens on an error path.\n\n## Details\n\nIn `src/library/tools/src/gramRd.y`, `TEXT_PUSH` moves a growing token from a stack array into a `malloc()`ed buffer, which the lexer functions free only on a normal return. In `mkMarkup()`, `install()` (via `KeywordLookup()`) rejects an over-long name with \"variable names are limited to 10000 bytes\" after the buffer has grown. r90723 (PR#19184) covers the bison stacks and pushback buffer, not this one. OSS-Fuzz's `rd` target found it at r90940; R 4.6.1 behaves the same.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `rd-token-buffer-leak-repro.R`; resident memory grows by 79 MB over 5000 failed parses in R 4.6.1 and by 0 MB with the patch.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `rd-token-buffer-leak.diff` (against r90959). `TEXT_PUSH` allocates with `R_alloc()` and each lexer function brackets its token with `vmaxget()`/`vmaxset()`, as `xxusermacro()` already does; `gramRd.c` is regenerated with bison 3.8.2. A `tests/reg-fuzz.R` test is included; `make check` passes and LeakSanitizer reports no leak.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/522, where CI results can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while investigating an OSS-Fuzz report for the `rd` fuzz target.",
